\title{Odd squares, again}
\date{2026-02-11T14:20:53Z}
\tag{discrete}
\taxon{Example}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{Prove: #{\all{n}{\Z} \Odd(n) \iff \Odd(n^2).}}

\proof{
  Let #{k} be an arbitrary integer; we will prove #{\Odd(k) \iff \Odd(k^2)}.  To prove this biconditional, we will prove both directions.

  \subproof{
    #{\Odd(k) \to \Odd(k^2)}.  We [already proved this above](005L).
  }

  \subproof{
  #{\Odd(k^2) \to \Odd(k)}.

    \p{Suppose #{\Odd(k^2)}, that is, suppose there is an integer #{j} such that #{k^2 = 2j+1}.  Then we must show #{\Odd(k)}, that is, we must pick an integer #{p} such that #{k = 2p + 1}.
    \subproof{
      Here is where, starting from our assumption that #{k^2 = 2j+1}, we should pick an integer #{p} so that #{k = 2p+1}.  But we are kind of stuck: knowing something about #{k^2} does not really help us conclude anything about #{k}.  
    }
    }
  }
}

\p{Let's try again.  Instead of directly proving #{\Odd(k^2) \implies \Odd(k)}, let's prove its [contrapositive](003W), which [we know is logically equivalent](003X).  The contrapositive is #{\neg \Odd(k) \implies \neg \Odd(k^2)}, but we will assume the following theorem for now:

\transclude{005S}

(Check: do you see why #{\neg \Odd(n)} and #{\Even(n)} are not literally the same?  We will be able to formally prove this later in the semester.)}

\proof{
  #{\Odd(k^2) \implies \Odd(k)}.  We will instead prove the contrapositive, that is, #{\neg \Odd(k) \implies \neg \Odd(k^2)}, which is equivalent to #{\Even(k) \implies \Even(k^2)}.  So suppose #{\Even(k)}, then we must show #{\Even(k^2)}.

  \subproof{
    Since we know #{\Even(k)}, [by definition](004V) that means there exists some integer #{j} such that #{k = 2j}.  To show #{\Even(k^2)}, we must pick an integer #{p} such that #{k^2 = 2p}.  But since #{k = 2j}, then #{k^2 = (2j)^2 = 4j^2 = 2(2j^2)}, so we can pick #{p = 2j^2}.
  }
}
